---
import { JIRA, PATHS, ROLE_LIMITS, ROLES } from '@lib/compare/jira-alternative';
---

<section id="ja-roles" class="border-t border-line" aria-labelledby="ja-roles-title">
  <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-24 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 lg:gap-12">
    <div class="lg:col-span-5">
      <h2 id="ja-roles-title" class="h2">
        Five roles, decided before you arrive.
      </h2>
      <p class="prose mt-5 max-w-[46ch]">
        There is no role editor and no permission scheme to design. Each person has an organisation role and a role in
        each project, and the same matrix answers the app, the API and every agent.
      </p>
      <p class="prose mt-4 max-w-[46ch]">
        That is a trade, not a shortcut to every permission need. Jira’s{' '}
        <a class="link font-normal" href={JIRA.permissions} rel="noopener">
          permission schemes
          <span class="arr ext" aria-hidden="true">
            ↗
          </span>
        </a>{' '}
        and{' '}
        <a class="link font-normal" href={JIRA.workflows} rel="noopener">
          workflow conditions
          <span class="arr ext" aria-hidden="true">
            ↗
          </span>
        </a>{' '}
        go much further.
      </p>
      <a class="link text-sm mt-6 inline-block" href={PATHS.permissions}>
        The full permission matrix
        <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">
          →
        </span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 space-y-10">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium">The five roles</h3>
        <dl class="scopes mt-3">
          {ROLES.map(item => (
            <>
              <dt>{item.role}</dt>
              <dd>{item.can}</dd>
            </>
          ))}
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-medium">Deliberately not built</h3>
        <ul class="rule-list mt-3">
          {ROLE_LIMITS.map(item => (
            <li class="py-4 prose text-base">{item}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
